.TH WHOIS 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
whois \- ask a whois server about a domain name
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B whois
.RB [ \-i ]
.RB [ \-h
.IR server ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR port ]
.IR name ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B whois
asks a whois server about each
.IR name ,
in order, and writes the replies to standard output.
It starts at
.BR whois.iana.org ,
or at the server given with
.BR \-h .
When a reply names another server to ask,
.B whois
asks that server too, and by default shows only the last reply.
.PP
The names are meant to be domain names; see
.BR NOTES .
.SS Queries
.B whois
connects to the server over TCP, sends the
.I name
as given followed by CR LF, and reads the reply until the server closes
the connection.
A read error, such as a reset connection, ends the reply the same way,
with no message.
Each
.I name
is a separate query.
.PP
A reply is expected to contain a line that begins with
.B domain:
or
.BR "domain name:" ,
ignoring case and leading white space.
If it contains none,
.B whois
connects to the same server again and sends
.B domain
and a space before the name.
This happens at most once per server, and the first reply is discarded.
.SS Redirects
Following a line that begins with
.B domain:
or
.BR "domain name:" ,
the first line that begins with
.B whois server:
or
.BR whois: ,
ignoring case and leading white space, names the next server.
The server is the rest of that line, without white space around it and in
lowercase, and may carry a port as described for
.BR \-h .
.PP
An empty server is followed too.
IANA gives one for a top-level domain with no whois server.
Windows resolves the empty name to an address of the local computer, so
the query usually fails with
.BR "can\(aqt connect to remote host" ,
and without
.B \-i
no reply is shown.
.PP
.B whois
asks the new server about the same name on port 43, whatever
.B \-p
says.
A line that names the server just asked, compared as text, is ignored,
and its reply is shown.
Other lines, such as
.BR "Registrar WHOIS Server:" ,
are not followed.
There is no limit to the number of redirects, so servers that name each
other make
.B whois
loop until it is interrupted.
.SS Output
For each query,
.B whois
writes these lines to standard output:
.TP
\fB[Querying \fIhost\fB:\fIport\fB \(aq\fIname\fB\(aq]\fR
Before it connects.
.I host
is the server as given, and
.I port
is the
.B \-p
port, or 43.
A port in
.I host
overrides it, but both are shown.
.I name
is what is sent, without the CR LF, so a second query shows the
.B domain
prefix.
.TP
\fB[Redirected to \fIhost\fB]\fR
When the reply names another server.
.TP
\fB[\fIhost\fB]\fR
Followed by the text of the reply, with
.I host
as in the first line.
The text of a reply that names another server is not shown, except with
.BR \-i .
.PP
Each line of the reply text ends with LF.
A line ends at its first CR or LF, so text after a CR inside a line is
lost.
A line of 2046 bytes or more is broken after every 2046 bytes, each piece
ended with LF, so a line of exactly 2046 bytes is followed by an empty one.
A last line with no line end gets one.
.PP
.B whois
stops reading a reply after the line that brings it to 32768 bytes or
more, closes the connection, and drops the rest.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-ih ,
and the argument of
.B \-h
or
.B \-p
may be attached or be the next argument.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
Options may follow the operands unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
There are no long options.
.B \-h
names a server and does not print help.
.TP
.B \-i
Show the reply of every server asked, each after its own
.BI [ host ]
line, instead of only the last.
.TP
.BI "\-h " server
Ask
.I server
first, instead of
.BR whois.iana.org .
It is a name, an IPv4 address, or an IPv6 address.
A port may follow a name or IPv4 address after a colon, as in
.BR example.com:4343 ,
or an IPv6 address in brackets, as in
.BR [::1]:4343 ,
and overrides
.BR \-p .
An IPv6 address with more than one colon is never split this way.
A port must be a number from 0 to 65535.
.TP
.BI "\-p " port
Connect to
.I port
on the first server.
The default is 43.
It is a number from 0 to 2147483647, and only its low 16 bits are used:
65536 is port 0 and 70000 is port 4464.
It is not used for servers named by redirects.
A service name is not accepted.
.PP
An unknown option or a missing option argument prints a message and the
usage on standard error.
A missing
.I name
prints only the usage, and an invalid
.B \-p
value prints only a message.
Each exits with status 1.
When the first argument is
.BR \-\-help ,
the usage is printed on standard output instead, and the exit status
is 0.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
When set, the first operand ends the options, so a later
.B \-i
is taken as a name.
The C library's option parser reads it.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
If the first of these that is set has the value
.BR C ,
output to a console is not converted, as described in
.BR NOTES ,
and the console interprets the bytes in its own code page.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every name was queried, whether or not the server knew it, or the usage
was requested with
.BR \-\-help .
.TP
.B 1
An invalid command line; a server that could not be resolved or whose
port is invalid; or a connection that could not be made.
The message is on standard error, prefixed with
.BR whois: .
Output already written stays, and the names after the failing query are
not asked about.
.TP
.B 141
Writing to standard output failed because it is a pipe that was closed.
Output to a pipe is buffered in 4 KiB blocks, and a failed write of the
last block, at exit, is ignored, so a closed pipe gives status 0 when the
output fits in one block.
.SH EXAMPLES
Look up a domain, and show how the query reached the registry:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ whois kernel.org | head \-n 4
[Querying whois.iana.org:43 \(aqkernel.org\(aq]
[Redirected to whois.publicinterestregistry.org]
[Querying whois.publicinterestregistry.org:43 \(aqkernel.org\(aq]
[whois.publicinterestregistry.org]
.EE
.RE
.PP
Show every server's reply, but only the bracketed lines:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ whois \-i kernel.org | grep \(aq^\e[\(aq
[Querying whois.iana.org:43 \(aqkernel.org\(aq]
[whois.iana.org]
[Redirected to whois.publicinterestregistry.org]
[Querying whois.publicinterestregistry.org:43 \(aqkernel.org\(aq]
[whois.publicinterestregistry.org]
.EE
.RE
.PP
Ask a registry directly instead of starting at IANA:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ whois \-h whois.verisign\-grs.com example.com
.EE
.RE
.PP
Ask a server on another port, giving the port with the server:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ whois \-h \(aq[::1]:4343\(aq example.com
.EE
.RE
.PP
There is no timeout.
Give up after 20 seconds with
.BR timeout :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ timeout 20 whois example.com
.EE
.RE
.PP
Look up an IP address, which
.B whois
cannot do, by sending the query with
.BR nc .
The
.B sleep
holds the input open, since without it the reply is often cut short:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ { printf \(aq8.8.8.8\er\en\(aq; sleep 5; } | nc whois.arin.net 43
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from other whois clients:
.IP \(bu 2
The only options are
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-h ,
and
.BR \-p .
There is none to stop redirects or to skip the second query.
.IP \(bu 2
Only a reply with a
.B domain:
or
.B domain name:
line is followed to another server.
.PP
A name that is not a domain name, such as an IP address or an AS
number, normally gets a reply with no such line.
Then the second query, with
.B domain
before the name, is sent, and only its reply, usually an error, is shown.
Send such queries with
.BR nc (1)
instead, as shown in
.BR EXAMPLES .
.PP
.B whois
uses TCP port 43 and sends the query unencrypted.
There is no proxy support.
There is no timeout except the one Windows applies to making a
connection, and
.B whois
waits for the server to close the connection.
.PP
Windows resolves server names, as for
.BR nc (1).
When a name has several addresses,
.B whois
tries only one: the first IPv4 address, or else the first address.
Redirect targets are resolved the same way.
.PP
Socket failures are reported without the Windows reason, as in
.BR "whois: can\(aqt connect to remote host (127.0.0.1)" .
The address is shown only for IPv4.
A server name that fails to resolve, or that is malformed, is reported as
\fBwhois: bad address \(aq\fIserver\fB\(aq\fR,
and an invalid port in it as
\fBwhois: bad port \(aq\fIport\fB\(aq\fR.
.PP
When standard output is a console, the reply is converted for display
from the ANSI code page, which is UTF-8 on Windows 10 version 1903 and
later.
Escape sequences in a reply, such as those for color, take effect, and
busybox emulates them on consoles without virtual terminal support.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR nc (1),
.BR timeout (1)
